Article 17

1. To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall empower and build awareness among families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows:a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in formal, non-formal, and informal education;
b. provision of accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. creating a friendly and safe work environment atmosphere for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing an information system that is easily accessible;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children.

Article 18

1. Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 17 shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population and civil registration;
f. law;
g. women's empowerment and child protection;
h. cooperatives and micro enterprises;
i. mental and spiritual; and
j. peace and order.
k. Community and village empowerment.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 19

1. In the context of providing protection services to women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government shall establish UPTDPPA. (2) UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to implement services for:
a. public complaints;
b. victim outreach;
c. case management;
d. temporary shelter;
e. mediation; and
f. victim assistance.
2. Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.
3. To assist in the protection of women and children, the Regional Government may establish:
a. Task Force for Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for Prevention of Child Marriage;
c. Task Force for Child-Friendly Regency or other designation;
d. Regional Action Committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
e. West Pesisir Regional Children's Forum;
f. Community-Based Integrated Child Protection (PATBM).

Article 21

Forms of services for victims organized by UPTD PPA include:
a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services (visum);
c. psychosocial services;
d. legal services;
e. economic independence services; and
f. spiritual services.

Article 24

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protection of women and children, cooperation may be conducted with:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Governments;
d. Non-Governmental Institutions;
e. Community;
f. Universities; and
g. Mass Media.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. recovery of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration;
d. provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. followed up in accordance with applicable law.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a Cooperation Agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER VIII

ASSISTANCE

Article 26

1. Assistance includes all integrated efforts to restore and strengthen the condition of victims, conducted by health workers, education, police, prosecutors, courts, advocates, social workers, volunteer assistants and spiritual counselors.
2. Assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The duties of assistants are:
a. providing sufficient information to victims about their rights;
b. providing psychosocial services to victims so that victims feel safe and comfortable;
c. accompanying victims during the examination and medical recovery process;
d. accompanying victims during the medicolegal process;
e. accompanying victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
f. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
g. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by mass media;
h. coordinating with other assistants; and
i. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

CHAPTER IX

CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY

Article 27

1. In the context of implementing the Convention on the Rights of the Child and various laws and regulations as well as policies related to the fulfillment of children's rights and special protection of children, the Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions make efforts to realize a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. To realize a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1), child-friendly sub-districts and child-friendly villages/sub-districts must be established.
3. The Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions incorporated in the Task Force in realizing a child-friendly regency must pay attention to children's rights related to the right to life, growth and development, protection and participation.
4. The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (3) is responsible for the fulfillment of children's rights related to:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, use of leisure time and cultural arts activities;
e. special protection of children.
5. Further provisions regarding Child-Friendly Regency, child-friendly sub-districts and child-friendly villages/sub-districts are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 29

1. Funding for protection of women and children victims of violence includes:
a. medical services, which include doctor's examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, medication costs and medical support costs;
b. medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and Visum et Psikiatrikum;
c. psychosocial services, consisting of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. legal services;
e. economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
2. Funding management is carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government which is conducted transparently and can be legally accounted for.

CHAPTER XI

PROVISIONS ON SANCTIONS

Article 30

1. Officials who do not carry out their obligations and responsibilities as referred to in Article 14, shall be subject to administrative action and/or sanctions.
2. Administrative action and/or sanctions as referred to in paragraph (1) are in the form of:
a. Verbal warning;
b. Written warning;
c. Written statement of dissatisfaction;
d. Deduction of performance allowance by 25% (twenty-five percent) for 12 months.
3. Officials as referred to in paragraph (1) are the Head of the PPPAKB Office or those in charge of the protection of women and children.
